---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";

// STARTER_CONTENT: replace the hero artwork and its alt text below, then
// remove this comment.
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A lecture theatre reduced to flat gold and black shapes, rows of seats sweeping past the frame, in a two-ink risograph print"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will build a language. Not a vocabulary list and a font — a language
    with a phoneme inventory you can pronounce, rules about which sounds may
    sit next to which, a morphology, a word order, a lexicon that generates
    itself from its own roots, a writing system, and a society whose speakers
    leave certain things unsaid because everyone already knows them.
  </p>
  <p>
    It is built one subsystem a week, in order, and the order is not
    negotiable: week six is impossible without week two. Every week has a film
    attached and the screening is compulsory, because each one is the way into
    the week's problem — Frommer's ejectives are how you learn what a working
    actor can be asked to pronounce, and <cite>Arrival</cite> is screened twice
    because the film that teaches you phonology in February is the one worth
    disagreeing with about meaning in April.
  </p>
  <p>
    In week twelve you read twelve lines aloud, at speaking pace, in a language
    that did not exist in February. Then somebody who has never seen it is
    handed one sentence and asked to read it back.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Final-year students who want a semester of systematic building rather than
    a semester of reading about it. No prior linguistics is assumed and none is
    required; you will be taught the IPA in week two and the interlinear gloss
    in week four, which is everything the course needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    What is assumed is that you will watch eleven films, turn up on Thursdays,
    and be willing to say something out loud that you have only ever written
    down. If the idea of pronouncing your own invented phoneme in front of nine
    people is the part you would want to skip, this is not the elective for
    you — that is the assessment.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each teaching session.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
